package cajeta.io.net;

import cajeta.lang.String;

/**
 * The server's **max-concurrent-connections cap** was reached and the
 * active [`LoadShedPolicy`](LoadShedPolicy.cajeta) is
 * [`REFUSE`](LoadShedPolicy.cajeta#REFUSE) — so the surplus connection is
 * shed rather than queued (plan item **NET-4.4**). Raised by
 * [`ConnectionLimiter.admitOrThrow`](ConnectionLimiter.cajeta#admitOrThrow)
 * when [`tryAdmit`](ConnectionLimiter.cajeta#tryAdmit) finds no free
 * permit; the accept loop catches it, closes the just-accepted socket, and
 * keeps serving — the excess load is shed back to the client, which sees an
 * immediate connection close. The (cap+1)th connection in
 * `ServerTests.connectionCapShedsExcess`.
 *
 * ## Why a `NetException`, not an OS-errno subtype
 *
 * Unlike [`ConnectionRefusedException`](ConnectionRefusedException.cajeta)
 * (`ECONNREFUSED` — the *kernel* refused a `connect`), this is an
 * **application-level admission decision**: the OS accepted the socket
 * fine; *our* limiter chose to shed it. There is therefore no platform
 * errno behind it, so it carries
 * [`kind == NetException.KIND_OTHER`](NetException.cajeta#KIND_OTHER) (99) —
 * the "no specific platform cause" ordinal. It still descends from
 * [`NetException`](NetException.cajeta) so a request-boundary
 * `catch (NetException e)` net-out covers it, and so a server operator can
 * declare it in a `throws` clause when surfacing capacity events.
 *
 * The `cap` field carries the configured
 * [`maxConnections`](ConnectionLimits.cajeta#maxConnections) ceiling that
 * was hit, so an operator (or a `503` responder) can report exactly which
 * limit to raise — the same `(message, <numeric ceiling>)` shape
 * [`HeadersTooLargeException`](http/HeadersTooLargeException.cajeta) uses
 * to carry its crossed limit in a field rather than baking it into the
 * message string.
 */
public class TooManyConnectionsException extends NetException {
    /**
     * The configured concurrent-connection ceiling
     * ([`ConnectionLimits.maxConnections`](ConnectionLimits.cajeta#maxConnections))
     * that was reached when this connection was shed.
     */
    public int32 cap;

    public TooManyConnectionsException(#String message, int32 cap) {
        this.message = message;
        this.cause = 0;
        this.kind = 99;   // NetException.KIND_OTHER — app-level cap, no errno
        this.cap = cap;
    }
}
